from author import ALL
EXTRAS={
'environment':'''A notebook is useful for inspecting one experiment, but execution order can hide dependencies: a cell may succeed only because an earlier cell created a variable. Move the analyzer into an importable function and run it from a fresh interpreter. Keep a small notebook as a consumer of that function rather than a second implementation.\n\nUse a virtual environment per project and install from a reviewed manifest. Record Python and package versions with the run artifact; a requirements range alone does not recreate a particular installation. Store a tested lock or freeze after validation. Verify setup with an import smoke test and the analyzer's behavior tests before connecting a provider. A key belongs in the local process environment and must be excluded from Git, browser bundles and progress backups.''',
'python-values':'''A dictionary maps stable test IDs to records; a set records membership such as already-seen IDs; a list preserves an ordered event stream. Choosing a set for the event stream discards ordering and duplicates, which can hide repeated failures. A tuple can represent a fixed composite identity such as tenant, build and test.\n\nMutability matters when fixtures are shared. Assigning second = first does not copy a list, and a shallow dictionary copy still shares nested lists. A test that appends to a nested fixture can change later tests. Build a fresh fixture for each test or copy the exact mutable boundary. Test duplicates, absent keys and unexpected field types, and avoid using a mutable default argument such as failures=[] in a function signature.''',
'python-control':'''A comprehension is a transformation, not a substitute for readable error handling. Collect failed records with a clear predicate, then compute summaries separately. If a status is missing, decide explicitly whether to reject the record or label it unknown; silently treating it as passed changes the denominator.\n\nShort-circuit operators return operands, which can be useful but surprising: value or default replaces valid falsy values such as zero. A zero duration is different from a missing duration. Use is None when absence is the intended condition. Test an empty run list, all passes, all failures, missing fields and zero durations. These cases establish the analyzer's contract independently of the large synthetic dataset.''',
'python-functions':'''Function contracts should name accepted input, returned shape and failure behavior. A pure summarize function receives records and returns metrics without opening files or reading environment variables. A thin command-line wrapper owns file loading and report writing. This separation makes malformed-file tests different from aggregation tests.\n\nAvoid global mutable counters and hidden configuration, because tests then depend on execution order. Use keyword-only arguments for optional policies such as unknown_status='reject', and document when an exception is raised. A decorator can add timing while preserving the wrapped function's metadata with functools.wraps, but it must return the result and propagate errors. Verify both success and failure paths after wrapping; observability should not change the domain contract.''',
'python-production':'''Runtime validation must distinguish coercion from a strict contract. A duration supplied as the string '2.4' may be intentionally accepted by an ingestion adapter, but a tool's write approval should never interpret arbitrary nonempty strings as true. Validate boundaries according to the domain and preserve rejected records with safe error categories.\n\nUse pathlib for explicit fixture roots and context managers for file handles. Validate parsed JSON structure after parsing: syntactically valid JSON can still be an array where a dictionary is required. Write reports to a temporary file and replace the target after the complete artifact is ready, preventing readers from observing partial output. Test missing files, invalid encoding, malformed JSON and valid-but-wrong types separately.''',
'python-tests':'''A useful test must fail when the behavior it protects is broken. For the analyzer, independently compute a tiny expected result rather than calling the same helper used by production code to create the expectation. Change failed to passed in one input and confirm only the intended count changes.\n\nParametrize boundary cases and use fixtures to provide fresh records. Monkeypatch environment variables and mock network adapters at the application's dependency boundary, not inside the function under test. Property-based testing can generate many inputs for invariants such as 0 <= pass_rate <= 1 and failure_count <= total_count, but those invariants do not prove the exact formula. Combine broad properties with hand-computed examples and targeted counterexamples.''',
'numpy-arrays':'''For a duration table shaped [builds, tests], axis=0 aggregates across builds and axis=1 aggregates across tests. Keep axis names in comments or variable names because an array does not retain the meaning of its coordinates automatically. A scalar, row vector and column vector have different broadcasting behavior.\n\nBoolean masks select elements satisfying a condition; using a mask with the wrong shape can fail or select the wrong subset. Check dtype and finite values before computing statistics. Integer arrays are appropriate for IDs but floating-point arrays are needed for normalized metrics. Test a one-row table, a one-column table, empty arrays and NaN values. Compare vectorized output with a small explicit loop as an independent oracle.''',
'numpy-cosine':'''Raw dot product rewards magnitude as well as direction. For a=[1,0], b=[10,1] and c=[1,0], b has a larger dot product than c but lower cosine similarity. This difference matters when an index is configured for dot distance while embeddings are not normalized.\n\nA zero vector has no direction. The teaching implementation returns a declared zero score, while another application may reject it; either policy must be explicit and tested. Reject mismatched dimensions rather than letting zip silently truncate coordinates. In retrieval, keep document identity aligned with vector rows, and test identical, opposite, orthogonal and zero vectors. Geometric similarity remains evidence for ranking, not proof that two failure causes are the same.''',
'numpy-sampling':'''A random seed reproduces a pseudo-random sequence under a fixed implementation and data order; it does not make a sample representative. Uniform row sampling can underrepresent rare product defects or repeat many rows from one incident. Stratification preserves selected class proportions, while group sampling avoids splitting closely related incidents across partitions.\n\nReport which sampling unit was used: run, test, incident or build. For uncertainty estimates over correlated runs, resample independent incidents rather than individual repeated observations. Keep a sealed holdout that was not inspected during iteration. Test that sampled IDs are valid, groups are disjoint where required and every necessary class has coverage. Reproducibility and representativeness are separate evaluation properties.''',
'pandas-clean':'''Missing data needs a domain interpretation before imputation. A missing duration may indicate a cancelled test; filling it with zero can make an outage look like a performance improvement. Preserve a missingness indicator and distinguish not-run from passed. Stable test IDs are safer join keys than display names that may be renamed.\n\nCheck duplicates on the intended identity, such as tenant, build and test, rather than dropping identical rows indiscriminately. A legitimate rerun has a different run ID and may be required to measure flakiness. Preserve raw records, clean in a reproducible function and count rejected or changed rows. Test malformed durations, duplicate identities and missing statuses, and verify that cleaning does not silently change the total population used for pass-rate metrics.''',
'pandas-group':'''A per-module pass rate needs the correct denominator. Excluding cancelled or missing-status rows may be a defensible policy, but report that exclusion; otherwise a module with many unexecuted tests can appear healthy. Weighted overall pass rate differs from the mean of module rates when modules have different numbers of runs.\n\nFlakiness is not simply any test that ever failed. A candidate may show mixed pass/fail outcomes under comparable builds, but environment changes and product fixes can also explain the variation. Group by stable test identity, order by time and retain build context. Test all-pass, all-fail, mixed outcomes and one-run tests. Label the result a flaky-test candidate until the controlled reproduction establishes nondeterministic behavior.''',
'pandas-joins':'''A many-to-many join multiplies rows. If a test appears twice in both tables, the joined result contains four combinations; a later mean or count can be wrong even though no exception occurred. Use validate='many_to_one' when enriching runs with a single testcase record, and inspect unmatched keys with an indicator column.\n\nParse timestamps with an explicit timezone and sort within the test group before calculating lagged duration features. Input row order is not a chronological guarantee. A future build's duration must not become a feature for an earlier prediction. Test duplicate enrichment keys, unmatched IDs, tied timestamps and timezone conversion. Compare row counts before and after the join and retain the reason for every intended change.''',
'sql-basics':'''WHERE filters rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation. COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(duration) excludes NULL durations. AVG also ignores NULL, which can conceal missing execution data if the report does not include a missing count. Use CASE to make denominator policy explicit.\n\nOrdering is a contract when comparing query output in a test. Without ORDER BY, a database may return equivalent rows in a different order. Test all-pass, all-fail and NULL-status cases with hand-computed expected rows. Relational models should use stable keys and constraints for builds, cases, runs and defects; a test display name is a useful label but a weak primary key when names can change.''',
'sql-joins':'''A LEFT JOIN retains every left-side record, but a WHERE predicate on a right-side column can remove unmatched rows and effectively turn the result into an inner join. Put matching conditions in ON when unmatched cases must remain visible, and explicitly decide how NULL evidence affects the report.\n\nUse EXISTS for membership questions when joined detail rows would multiply results. UNION removes duplicate rows; UNION ALL preserves them and is appropriate only when duplicate events should count. Test one-to-many failures, unmatched defects and duplicate related records. Explain expected row cardinality before running the query, then compare actual counts. A plausible report number is not enough if the join changed the unit of analysis.''',
'sql-windows':'''A latest-result query needs a deterministic tie break. ROW_NUMBER over partition by test_id order by build_id descending, run_id descending identifies one latest run; RANK can return several ties. LAG and LEAD preserve rows while accessing neighbors within the declared order.\n\nThree failures in a row is different from three failures anywhere in history. Filter-free ordered windows must preserve intervening passes; filtering failures before LAG creates a false streak. Tests with F,P,F,F and F,F,F make the distinction visible. Inspect plans to understand scans, sorts and joins, and evaluate indexes for the actual filter/order workload rather than assuming an index always improves performance.''',
'stats-descriptive':'''Mean and median describe center, while variance and standard deviation describe spread. Percentiles describe thresholds in an ordered sample under a stated convention. A latency report should include sample count, failure count and tail statistics because dropping timed-out requests can make an outage appear faster.\n\nPopulation variance divides by N when describing the full declared population; sample variance often uses N−1 when estimating population variance under appropriate assumptions. Neither formula fixes a biased sample. Compare cold and warm requests, input lengths and concurrency before attributing a latency shift to a model change. Use a small hand-computed sample to test calculations and retain raw durations for investigating tail events.''',
'stats-probability':'''Suppose only 1% of runs are true product defects, a detector finds 90% of them, and it alerts on 5% of non-defects. Among 10,000 runs, about 90 alerts are true positives and 495 are false positives, so positive predictive value is only 90/585, about 15.4%. High sensitivity does not imply trustworthy alerts when the base rate is low.\n\nConditional probabilities depend on what is being conditioned on: P(alert|defect) differs from P(defect|alert). Bernoulli models describe one binary outcome and binomial models count independent trials with a common success probability. Related CI retries violate independence, so choose the sampling unit carefully. Test the Bayes calculation with explicit counts and consider human-review capacity when setting alert thresholds.''',
'stats-bootstrap':'''Bootstrap intervals depend on the data representing the target population and on the resampling unit. Resampling individual retries from the same incident treats correlated observations as independent and can make intervals too narrow. A grouped bootstrap samples incidents, retaining their associated observations together.\n\nA fixed seed permits a reproducible teaching result but does not establish interval coverage for every distribution or tiny sample. Compare paired candidate-minus-baseline scores within each case before resampling paired differences. Report effect size and interval, not only whether zero is excluded. Test one observation, constant observations and a skewed sample; explain which conclusions remain uncertain when the dataset is small or unrepresentative.'''}
for l in ALL:
 if l['id'] in EXTRAS:l['sections'].append({'title':'Engineering boundaries and independent checks','paragraphs':EXTRAS[l['id']].split('\n\n')})
